## Filesystem Hierarchy (FHS)

FHS = **Filesystem Hierarchy Standard** — defines how directories are organized in Linux.

Everything starts from `/` (root). There are no drive letters like Windows.

| Directory | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/` | Root of the entire filesystem |
| `/bin` | Essential user commands (`ls`, `cp`, `mv`) |
| `/sbin` | System/admin commands (`fdisk`, `reboot`) |
| `/boot` | Boot files: kernel, initramfs, GRUB config |
| `/dev` | Device files (`/dev/sda`, `/dev/null`) |
| `/etc` | Configuration files (`/etc/passwd`, `/etc/hosts`) |
| `/home` | Home directories for normal users |
| `/root` | Home directory for the root user |
| `/lib` | Shared libraries needed by binaries |
| `/media` | Auto-mount point for removable drives (USB, CD) |
| `/mnt` | Temporary manual mount point |
| `/opt` | Optional/third-party software |
| `/proc` | Virtual filesystem — process and kernel info |
| `/sys` | Hardware and kernel interface |
| `/tmp` | Temporary files (cleared on reboot) |
| `/var` | Variable data: logs (`/var/log`), cache, mail |
| `/usr` | User programs and data (`/usr/bin`, `/usr/lib`) |

> **Key concept:** In Linux, everything is a file — including devices, processes, and sockets.

### FHS Interview Q&A

**Q: Difference between `/bin` and `/usr/bin`?**  
`/bin` holds essential commands needed during boot. `/usr/bin` holds regular user programs installed after boot.

**Q: Where are config files stored?** `/etc`

**Q: Where are system logs?** `/var/log`

**Q: Difference between `/home` and `/root`?**  
`/home` is for regular users. `/root` is the home directory of the superuser.

**Q: What is `/proc`?**  
A virtual filesystem that exposes real-time kernel and process information. Nothing on disk — generated dynamically.

